    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Plants and their insect herbivores comprise over half of all described nonmicrobial species and interact in ways that influence key community- and ecosystem-level processes. A prevalent pattern in nature is that herbivory –the consumption plant tissue or fluids by herbivores, particularly insects– varies across species. Researchers have developed hypotheses to explain and predict what determines such variation. The Apparency Hypothesis suggests that plant species that are more “apparent” (long-lived, large, evergreen) to herbivores have evolved stronger anti-herbivore defenses than not-so-apparent plants (short-lived, small, deciduous), but empirical tests of this hypothesis have yielded inconsistent results. Furthermore, research on this topic has focused largely on species belonging to different lineages, so information is limited regarding the variation across species within the same lineage. Sympatric oaks provide an opportunity to examine this knowledge lacuna. Oaks influence forest structure, biodiversity, and community composition in California ecosystems. A suite of anthropogenic stressors including habitat loss, invasive species, and climate change threaten California oaks. Understanding the patterns of herbivory in four sympatric California oak species can help inform the differences in susceptibility to insect attack and plant characteristics potentially related to their apparency: local relative abundance and phenology (evergreen/deciduous) underlying such differences. My work reveals that the most apparent (the most abundant and evergreen) Q. agrifolia had a lower proportion of herbivory damage by type (i.e., the type of herbivore responsible for such damage) than the less abundant and deciduous oaks (Q. douglasii, Q. lobata and Q. kelloggii). Herbivory rates and palatability assays using a generalist herbivore exhibited little interspecific variation. The more apparent Q. agrifolia had a higher carbon nitrogen ratio, lower water content, and tougher leaves than the three less apparent deciduous oak species, suggesting that Q. agrifolia has stronger anti-herbivore defenses than the other species. Pubescence, a trait potentially associated with ant-herbivore defense, did not consistently vary in relation to species apparency, suggesting pubescence may relate to environmental factors such as drought tolerance. These results partially support the apparency hypothesis and suggest that the most abundant oak, Q. agrifolia, is better defended against herbivory challenges, while Q. kelloggii, the rarest species, is most vulnerable to herbivores and thus a conservation priority.",
